= Unit 2 · Reduction and confluence

== Lecture 3 · Beta reduction and evaluation strategies
#focus[Distinguish reduction from evaluation, identify normal forms, and separate weak from strong normalization.]

*Recall and approach.* Recall full beta reduction may act anywhere, while a weak strategy does not reduce under abstractions.


#definition[Beta reduction][A beta redex is $(lambda x.t)u$, whose contraction is $t[x:=u]$. One-step beta reduction allows one such contraction at any position, including inside an abstraction. Write $t arrow.r_beta u$ and write $arrow.r_beta^*$ for its reflexive transitive closure. A beta normal form contains no beta redex.]

#example[Two routes through one computation][The term $(lambda x.x)((lambda y.y)z)$ can first contract the outer redex to $(lambda y.y)z$, then to $z$. Alternatively reduce the argument to $z$, then contract $(lambda x.x)z$. Both routes reach $z$, but they pass through different intermediate terms.]

#definition[Normalization distinctions][A term is weakly normalizing if some finite reduction sequence reaches a normal form. It is strongly normalizing if no infinite reduction sequence starts from it. A reduction strategy chooses which available redex to contract; it is additional structure beyond the unrestricted reduction relation.]

Let $Delta=lambda x.x x$ and $Omega=Delta Delta$. Contracting its root redex returns $Omega$, giving an infinite reduction. Yet $(lambda x.y)Omega$ has normal form $y$ by contracting the outer redex immediately. Reducing inside the argument forever gives an infinite path. Hence weak and strong normalization are genuinely different properties of a term.

Call-by-name evaluates the function position and substitutes an argument without first evaluating it. Call-by-value first evaluates the function and argument to values before contracting their application. Both are usually weak evaluation strategies, meaning they do not reduce under an abstraction. Their “finished values” need not be beta normal forms under unrestricted reduction.

#strategy[Check a root contraction with the substitution bound and lift the argument through surrounding syntax.]

#theorem[Reduction does not introduce new free variables][If $t arrow.r_beta u$, then $FV(u) subset.eq FV(t)$.]
#proof[For a root redex, the substitution bound gives $FV(s[x:=v]) subset.eq(FV(s)without\{x\})union FV(v)$, exactly the free variables of $(lambda x.s)v$. For a step within an application or abstraction, use the induction hypothesis for the changed subterm and the recursive equations for $FV$.]

#warning[An unrestricted reduction relation need not be deterministic. A deterministic evaluation strategy does not establish that every possible reduction order has the same behavior. Confluence, proved next, is the property that relates different choices.]
#example[A value can still contain a redex][The term $lambda z.(lambda x.x)z$ is a value under weak call-by-value because it is an abstraction. Under full beta reduction it steps inside its body to $lambda z.z$, so it is not a beta normal form. Replacing the body by $Omega$ gives a weak value with infinitely many full reductions.]

When reporting a computation, state both the allowed positions and the rule for choosing among them. A strategy can stop because it refuses to enter a body, even though the unrestricted relation has available steps there. This distinction matters when comparing implementation behavior with a theorem about all reductions.

#checkpoint("2.1", [Is $lambda z.Omega$ a value under a weak call-by-value strategy? Is it a beta normal form?], [It is a value because it is an abstraction, but it is not a beta normal form and is not strongly normalizing under full beta reduction.])
#takeaways[Whenever saying “reduces,” specify the relation or strategy. Values, normal forms, and strongly normalizing terms are different notions.]
