== Lecture 4 · Parallel reduction and Church–Rosser
#focus[Prove confluence using parallel reduction and derive uniqueness of normal forms without claiming termination.]

*Recall and approach.* Recall the substitution-composition lemma and the reflexive transitive closure of a relation.


#definition[Parallel reduction][Write $t => u$ for the relation generated by variable reflexivity, congruence under abstraction and application, and the rule: if $s=>s'$ and $v=>v'$, then $(lambda x.s)v => s'[x:=v']$. It can contract several existing redexes in one step.]

The relation is reflexive by structural induction. Every one-step beta reduction is a parallel step. Every parallel step is a finite sequence of beta steps: first reduce the component terms by induction, then contract the root redex if that parallel rule was used. Thus the two relations have the same reflexive transitive closure.

#strategy[Use parallel reduction to synchronize duplicated residual redexes, then transfer its diamond property to beta sequences.]

#theorem[Parallel substitution lemma][If $s=>s'$ and $u=>u'$, then $s[x:=u]=>s'[x:=u']$, with capture avoidance.]
#proof[Induct on the derivation of $s=>s'$. Variable and congruence rules follow directly from substitution's definition. In the redex rule, rename the binder fresh for the substituted terms, apply the induction hypotheses to its body and argument, then use the parallel redex rule. The substitution-composition identity from Lecture 2 identifies the resulting contractum with the required substituted term.]

Define the complete development $t^*$ recursively: variables remain themselves; develop an abstraction's body; for an application that is already a root redex $(lambda x.s)v$, use $s^*[x:=v^*]$; for any other application $s v$, use $s^*v^*$.

#theorem[Complete-development lemma][If $t=>u$, then $u=>t^*$. Consequently parallel reduction has the diamond property.]
#proof[Induct on the syntax of $t$, considering the parallel rule used. Variable and abstraction cases are immediate. If $t$ is not a root redex, its parallel step uses application congruence; the induction hypotheses develop both resulting components to their starred versions, and application congruence gives $t^*$. If $t=(lambda x.s)v$ and the step uses congruence, the result is $(lambda x.s')v'$; the induction hypotheses on $s,v$ followed by the parallel redex rule reach $s^*[x:=v^*]$. If it uses root contraction, the result is $s'[x:=v']$, and the parallel substitution lemma with those same induction hypotheses reaches the identical term. Thus any two parallel successors of $t$ both parallel-reduce to $t^*$.]

#theorem[Confluence of beta reduction][If $t arrow.r_beta^* u$ and $t arrow.r_beta^* v$, there is $w$ with $u arrow.r_beta^* w$ and $v arrow.r_beta^* w$. Hence beta normal forms are unique up to alpha equivalence.]
#proof[The diamond property extends to finite sequences by filling a rectangular grid of one-step diamonds, formally an induction on the two sequence lengths. Parallel and beta reduction have the same transitive closure, so the result transfers to beta reduction. If $u,v$ are normal forms, neither can take a genuine further beta step, so a common reduct forces them to be alpha equivalent.]

#example[Why the one-step diamond is the wrong target][In $(lambda x.x x)((lambda y.y)z)$, contracting the argument first permits one more step to $z z$. Contracting the outside first duplicates the argument redex and generally needs two ordinary steps to the same term. Thus a proof demanding that the two beta successors join by exactly one ordinary step would fail. Parallel reduction can contract both residual copies together, which is why its diamond property is better suited to the proof.]

For the conversion form of Church–Rosser, induct along a finite zigzag of forward and reversed beta steps. Maintain a common reduct with the initial endpoint; a forward step uses confluence to join the old common reduct with the new step, and a reversed step extends the new endpoint's path. This yields a common reduct for convertible terms.

#checkpoint("2.2", [Why does confluence not prove that $Omega$ has a normal form?], [It compares reduction paths when they exist; it gives no guarantee that any path terminates.])
#takeaways[Confluence is a compatibility theorem about branching computation. Normalization is a separate termination theorem.]
